    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "Followup to (i.e. a narrow edge case of) https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/41716 \n\n\n## Problem\n\n\nWith multicursor, treesitter `in` after `an` selects the wrong node at the **primary** cursor: it selects the node's first child instead of the child it came from. The other cursors are correct.\n\n`vim.treesitter._select` keeps one module-level history of which child `select_parent` came from. The multicursor selection preview and cascade replay `an` at each other cursor between the primary's keystrokes, and each replay resets that history to its own node. The primary's `in` then finds no history for its node and falls back to the first child.\n\nNote: the general problem is not specific to treesitter. Any Lua code that keeps module-level state across keystrokes sees the side-effects of the previous replay(s), because the replay sandbox cannot isolate arbitrary Lua state.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```\nnvim --clean\n:set filetype=lua\n```\n\nBuffer:\n\n```lua\nfoo(one, two)\nbaz(five, six)\n```\n\n1. `gg0ftQj0fs`: a cursor on `two`, the primary on `six`.\n2. `van`: selects `six` (ok).\n3. `an`: selects `(five, six)` (ok).\n4. `in`: the primary selects `five`.\n\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n\n\n`in` returns to `six`, as it does without cursors. The other cursor correctly returns to `two`.\n\n## Possible solutions\n\n\n- (untested): end each preview refresh with one more dry-run of the span at the primary's own session start. Shared state is then last written for the primary's context, as if the other cursors' replays had not run.\n\n",
    "closed_at": null,
